The diagonals of adjacent faces of a cube meet at a vertex. Each face diagonal has length √2 times the side, and the angle between two such diagonals from the same vertex can be found using the dot product in 3D coordinates. Placing the vertex at the origin, the two diagonals are vectors like (1,1,0) and (1,0,1). Their dot product is 1, and each has magnitude √2, so cos θ = 1/(√2·√2) = 1/2, giving θ = 60°.

